 1 big thing: Bloodbath 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep ; Chart: Pete Gannon/Axios Well, that party didn't last long.

 Nvidia shares plunged 14% and Anthropic shares surrendered most of their IPO gains today amid a brutal tech stock rout, as the AI boom veered closer to bust. Why it matters: Anthropic's record IPO has failed to save the flagging AI trade from the weight of declining confidence , tightening credit and a scandal at the world's most important chipmaker.

 The tech-heavy Nasdaq plunged 5.3% today. ‼️ Stunning stat: Nvidia — which operates at the center of the AI economy as a critical chips supplier, investor and cheerleader — has now lost more than $2.5 trillion in market cap from its all-time high on Oct. 6.

 The company is grappling with the fallout from the Jensen Huang scandal , plus fears that AI spending isn't sustainable. Today, Reuters reported that the SEC is investigating Nvidia's accounting practices. Anthropic shares tumbled 8.5% in their first full day of trading after Thursday's biggest-ever IPO.

 At $88.91, the stock sits just above its IPO price of $83 and far below its first-day high of $115.10, reached shortly after trading opened midday yesterday. 🗣️ What they're saying: "Right now the market is selling first and asking questions later," writes Michael Monaghan, partner at Founder ETFs.

 The bottom line: "'Anthropic's blockbuster IPO, which priced at a record valuation, quickly turned into a sentiment inflection point as shares slid sharply from the open, dragging the broader AI ecosystem lower," writes Mark Hackett, chief market strategist at Nationwide.

